.\" Man page for pam_vncpasswd(8)
.TH PAM_VNCPASSWD 8 "2026-02-19" "artdaq" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
pam_vncpasswd \- PAM module to authenticate against a VNC password file
.SH SYNOPSIS
In
.IR /etc/pam.d/<service> :
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
auth   [sufficient|required]   pam_vncpasswd.so   [\fIoptions\fR]
.fi
.RE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pam_vncpasswd
is a PAM authentication module that verifies a supplied password against
the obfuscated password stored in the user's VNC password file
(default
.IR ~/.vnc/passwd ).
The file format is compatible with
.BR tigervnc (1).
.PP
Only the first 8 bytes (the read\-write password) of the file are compared.
A read\-only password occupying bytes 8\(en15 in the TigerVNC format, if
present, is ignored.
.PP
The comparison is performed in constant time using an XOR accumulation loop
to prevent timing side\-channel attacks.
.PP
The module does not require elevated privileges.
Sensitive buffers are zeroed with
.BR explicit_bzero (3)
before the module returns.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fBfile=\fR\fIpath\fR
Override the password\-file path determined by the configuration hierarchy.
A leading
.B ~/
is expanded to the authenticating user's home directory as returned by
.BR getpwnam (3).
.TP
.B nullok
Permit empty (zero\-length) passwords.
By default an empty password always fails authentication.
.TP
.B debug
Write additional diagnostic messages (username, authentication result) to
.BR syslog (3)
at the
.B LOG_DEBUG
level.
No sensitive data is ever logged.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The location of the VNC password file is determined by consulting the
following sources in order.
Later entries override earlier ones.
.PP
.RS 4
.IP "1." 4
Compiled\-in default:
.I <home>/.vnc/passwd
.IP "2." 4
System default config:
.I /usr/share/vnc/vncpasswd.conf
.IP "3." 4
System admin config:
.I /etc/vnc/vncpasswd.conf
.IP "4." 4
User config:
.I <home>/.config/vnc/vncpasswd.conf
.IP "5." 4
Module argument
.B file=
(highest priority)
.RE
.PP
Configuration files use the following format:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
# comment
passwd_file = /path/to/passwd
.fi
.RE
.PP
Blank lines and lines beginning with
.B #
are ignored.
Whitespace around the
.B =
sign is optional.
A path beginning with
.B ~/
is expanded to the user's home directory.
.SH RETURN VALUES
.TP
.B PAM_SUCCESS
Authentication succeeded: the supplied password matches the stored password.
.TP
.B PAM_AUTH_ERR
Authentication failed: wrong password, or empty password without
.BR nullok .
.TP
.B PAM_AUTHINFO_UNAVAIL
The VNC password file does not exist, is a symbolic link (rejected by
.B O_NOFOLLOW
protection), or is shorter than 8 bytes.
.TP
.B PAM_USER_UNKNOWN
The user is unknown to the system or has no home directory recorded in the
password database.
.TP
.B PAM_BUF_ERR
Memory allocation failure.
.TP
.B PAM_AUTHTOK_ERR
Returned by
.B pam_sm_chauthtok
to indicate that in\-band password changes are not supported; use
.BR vncpasswd (1)
instead.
.SH SECURITY
.SS TOCTOU protection
The password file is opened with
.B O_NOFOLLOW
so that a symbolic link in the final path component is never followed.
After opening, the file is verified via
.BR fstat (2)
to be a regular file owned by the authenticating user.
A
.B LOG_WARNING
syslog message is emitted if the file is group\- or world\-readable.
.SS Constant\-time comparison
Stored and derived password bytes are compared using a bitwise XOR
accumulation loop.
No data\-dependent branches are present in the comparison.
.SS Memory hygiene
All sensitive buffers (stored and derived password bytes) are zeroed with
.BR explicit_bzero (3)
before the function returns, guarding against data leakage via freed memory.
.SS No elevated privileges
The module runs entirely in the calling process context and requires no
.B setuid
wrapper or privileged helper.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.vnc/passwd
Default VNC password file.
.TP
.I /usr/share/vnc/vncpasswd.conf
System\-installed default configuration.
.TP
.I /etc/vnc/vncpasswd.conf
System administrator configuration override.
.TP
.I ~/.config/vnc/vncpasswd.conf
Per\-user configuration override.
.SH EXAMPLES
Authenticate with VNC password first, fall back to system password:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
auth   sufficient   pam_vncpasswd.so
auth   required     pam_unix.so
.fi
.RE
.PP
Use a non\-default password file:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
auth   sufficient   pam_vncpasswd.so file=~/.config/weston/vncpasswd
.fi
.RE
.PP
Allow empty passwords and enable debug logging:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
auth   sufficient   pam_vncpasswd.so nullok debug
.fi
.RE
.PP
Typical
.I /etc/pam.d/weston\-remote\-access
for a system where users may have either a Kerberos or VNC password:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
auth   sufficient   pam_vncpasswd.so
auth   required     pam_krb5.so
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR vncpasswd (1),
.BR pam (8),
.BR pam.d (5),
.BR pam.conf (5),
.BR tigervnc (1)
